There are far better ways of exercising wisdom than wandering around
Weak (which has obvious and not-so-obvious downsides).  Searching
exercises wisdom... or is that just finding hidden doors/traps?

grep exercise *.c | grep A_WIS | grep TRUE | wc -l

shows 60 instances where wisdom is exercised throughout the code. 
This discounts the cases where the boolean second parameter is not a
straightforward TRUE/FALSE, but something to be evaluated, like
!cheapskate when chatting with the Oracle.

Likewise, there are 19 instances of STR exercising, 12 for CON and 34
for DEX using a similar search for these attributes (INT and CHR can't
be exercised, only gained via magical means).

